"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[62401],{662401:e=>{e.exports='### Latency Degradation Debugging Workflow\n\nWhen a user reports latency spikes or degradation, follow this structured workflow to diagnose the root cause:\n\n**Step 1: Locate the spike and get granular latency data**\n\nThere are two entry points but both lead to the same outcome — an identified spike time with a narrow custom range of granular latency data:\n\n- **If the user provides an approximate time** (e.g., "around Feb 11 2pm"):\n  - Use get_endpoint_metrics with a custom range centered on that time (~1-2h window, e.g. startTime="2026-02-11T13:00:00Z", endTime="2026-02-11T15:00:00Z") to get granular latency data\n  - Confirm the exact spike from the p50/p99 Max timestamps in the results\n\n- **If no time is given** (user just says "latency is bad"):\n  - Start with a wide search: get_endpoint_metrics with timeRange=\'24h\' or \'7d\'\n  - Find the Max timestamp from p50Latency and p99Latency to identify when the peak occurred\n  - Re-query with a narrow custom range (~1-2h) around that Max timestamp (e.g. startTime/endTime centered on the spike) to get granular data\n\nBoth paths end with: an identified spike time and a narrow custom range with granular latency data\n\n**Step 2: Understand the spike using supporting metrics, events, and config**\n\nNow gather context around the spike to understand WHY it happened:\n\n- **Traffic metrics** (from get_endpoint_metrics with the same startTime/endTime from Step 1): Check requestRate, numParallelRequests, numConcurrency to see if traffic or scaling changed during the spike\n- **Events** (from get_endpoint_events): Look for deployment events (ENDPOINT_UPDATE_EVENT, SERVED_ENTITY_CREATION_EVENT) near the spike time. These indicate a config or model change was deployed — the events API does not log what specifically changed, only that a deployment occurred.\n- **Configuration** (from get_endpoint_details view=\'config\'): Check max_provisioned_concurrency and current model version\n- **Inference table** (from get_inference_table with queryType="performance_analysis"): Use startTime/endTime matching the spike window from Step 1 to scope the query. This confirms whether individual request execution time increased (vs. queuing/infrastructure latency). If inference tables are not enabled, skip this step and proceed with metrics and events only.\n\n**Step 3: Apply the decision tree**\n\nIF requestRate/numParallelRequests increased significantly:\n  IF numConcurrency reached max_provisioned_concurrency:\n    → ROOT CAUSE: Max concurrency limit reached\n    → RECOMMEND: Increase max_provisioned_concurrency\n  ELSE IF numConcurrency is increasing:\n    → ROOT CAUSE: Traffic spike triggering autoscaling\n    → RECOMMEND: Wait for autoscaling to complete; consider increasing min concurrency for faster response\nELSE (traffic is stable, but latency increased):\n  IF inference table shows per-request execution time increased:\n    IF a deployment event (ENDPOINT_UPDATE_EVENT, SERVED_ENTITY_CREATION_EVENT) was found near the spike:\n      → ROOT CAUSE: Recent deployment changed serving behavior (model version, concurrency, workload size, or other config)\n      → RECOMMEND: Compare current config to expected state; consider rolling back if the deployment correlates with latency increase\n    ELSE:\n      → ROOT CAUSE: Model execution time increase (unknown cause)\n      → RECOMMEND: Enable tracing to collect debugging data; check for payload changes or upstream issues\n  ELSE IF inference table is not enabled:\n    → Cannot confirm per-request execution time\n    → RECOMMEND: Enable inference tables to get per-request latency data for future debugging; meanwhile check provisioned concurrency vs. traffic and review recent deployment events\n  ELSE (per-request execution time is stable):\n    → ROOT CAUSE: Likely queuing or infrastructure issue (execution time is normal but end-to-end latency is elevated)\n    → RECOMMEND: Escalate to Databricks support with the spike timeframe and metrics evidence\n\n**Step 4: Present findings**\n- Summarize the root cause with evidence (which metrics/events support the conclusion)\n- Provide specific, actionable recommendations\n- Suggest what to monitor going forward\n'}}]);
